      "modelAnswer": "It's like crediting someone's account back after an accidental overcharge, rather than pretending the original charge never happened — the record of both the original charge and the refund stay in history side by side.\n\n• Why there's nothing to roll back: the seat reservation already committed and became visible to the rest of the system the moment it happened, including potentially another student browsing the course at that exact moment — rolling back only works on changes that are still uncommitted and invisible to others.\n\n• What the compensator actually does instead: it runs a brand-new, separate operation that increments the available-seat count back up, creating its own \"seat released\" record, distinct from the earlier \"seat reserved\" record.\n\n• Why this counts as a new business transaction, not a rollback: it adds a balancing entry to history rather than erasing the original entry, exactly matching the course's description of a compensator reversing the effect of a committed step, not undoing an uncommitted one.\n\n• A common mistake is: deleting the log entry and pretending nothing happened erases history instead of balancing it, which is exactly what a rollback would do, not a compensator; retrying the charge doesn't address the reservation at all and doesn't reverse anything; and pausing the whole service for manual review isn't how an automated compensating transaction works.\n\nSo the answer is: the compensator releases the seat via a brand-new operation that adds a balancing \"seat released\" record, rather than erasing the earlier \"seat reserved\" record the way a rollback would."
