      "prompt": "Q10(d)\n\nThe team worries that between seat reservation and payment failure, another student could see the course as full and be turned away, even though the seat is about to be released. Which cost of the saga approach (compared to 2PC) does this illustrate, and why doesn't 2PC have this exact problem — even though it has its own different availability cost?",
      "modelAnswer": "It's like a shop assistant marking an item as 'sold' the instant you pick it off the shelf, before you've actually paid — if your card then gets declined, another customer who walked past in that moment already saw it as unavailable and left, even though the assistant is about to put it back on the shelf seconds later.\n\n• The saga's \"no isolation\" cost: because each step commits immediately and is visible right away, the in-between state — seat reserved, payment not yet confirmed — is visible to everyone else, including another student who could be shown \"full\" and turned away even though the seat is about to be released by the compensator.\n\n• Why 2PC avoids this exact problem: its Phase 1 prepare never actually applies or exposes the change — the reservation stays uncommitted and invisible to other students until the coordinator's global Phase 2 decision is made, so nobody ever sees a seat as taken only to have it reappear moments later.\n\n• 2PC's own, different cost: it holds locks across the whole cross-network exchange, blocking anyone else from touching that seat while the coordinator waits for every vote to come in — the course describes this as an \"unavailability protocol,\" since participants can be blocked indefinitely if the coordinator stalls.\n\n• A common mistake is: this isn't a durability failure, since nothing is lost, just temporarily visible in an in-between state; it isn't a one-off implementation bug, it's a structural property of committing each saga step immediately; and sagas aren't strictly worse in every respect, since 2PC trades this visibility problem for its own real availability cost (blocking).\n\nSo the answer is: this is the saga's \"no isolation\" cost — intermediate state is visible to others — while 2PC avoids it by never exposing the change until the final decision, at the cost of blocking everyone else via held locks instead."
