      "modelAnswer": "Service demand asks one simple question for each device: out of all the time it was busy, how much of that belongs to each completed job, no matter how many individual visits it took to rack up that busy time.\n\n• The formula: service demand = total busy time ÷ number of completed transactions in that period (not divided by the number of individual visits/calls).\n\n• Application server: 540 seconds busy ÷ 900 completed transactions = 0.6 seconds per transaction.\n\n• Database server: 720 seconds busy ÷ 900 completed transactions = 0.8 seconds per transaction (even though it took 1,800 individual calls to rack up that busy time).\n\n• Identifying the bottleneck: 0.8 > 0.6, so the database server has the higher total service demand and is the bottleneck, even though both devices are measured against the exact same 900 completed transactions.\n\n• A common mistake is: dividing busy time by the number of individual visits/calls (1,800) instead of completed transactions (900) answers a different question — the average time per visit, not the per-transaction demand that actually determines the bottleneck; multiplying the two numbers together doesn't correspond to any real physical quantity; and dividing transactions by busy time gives a rate, not a service demand, and reasoning from \"more calls per second\" alone skips the actual demand calculation.\n\nSo the answer is: application server demand = 0.6s/transaction, database server demand = 0.8s/transaction, and the database server is the bottleneck."
