      "modelAnswer": "N* is basically an estimate of the 'knee' in the throughput curve — the point where adding more terminals stops helping much and starts just adding wait time instead, found by comparing how much terminals think versus how much the bottleneck device itself can chew through.\n\n• The formula: N* = (D + Z) / Dmax, using total demand D = 1.4s, think time Z = 8s, and the bottleneck's own demand Dmax = 0.8s (the database server).\n\n• The calculation: N* = 9.4 / 0.8 ≈ 11.75, so about 12 terminals.\n\n• Where 10 terminals sits: just below the knee — still in the region where adding a few more terminals would roughly track a proportional throughput gain, but close enough that response time (5.33s from the earlier part) is already noticeably above the low-load floor (1.4s), signalling the system is approaching that knee.\n\n• How to raise N*: target the bottleneck device specifically. Since N* = (D+Z)/Dmax, reducing Dmax — speeding up or offloading the database server, e.g. a read replica or caching frequently-read data — raises both N* and the high-load ceiling (1/Dmax). Adding capacity anywhere else, like the application server, wouldn't move the knee at all, because the database server would remain the bottleneck regardless.\n\n• A common mistake is: multiplying instead of dividing, or dividing the wrong way round, both produce numbers that don't match the actual knee formula or make no physical sense as a terminal count; and reducing Z (think time) isn't something the enrolment office actually controls, nor does it change which device is the bottleneck.\n\nSo the answer is: N* ≈ 11.75 (about 12 terminals); 10 terminals is just below the knee, and raising N* requires reducing the bottleneck database server's own service demand."
