      "modelAnswer": "Think of each HTTP method as answering 'what happens if I do this exact same thing again?' Stapling a new sheet onto a pile (POST, create) gives you two sheets the second time; looking at a noticeboard (GET, view) never changes what's on it; rewriting today's date on a whiteboard (PUT, update) just leaves the same date there again; and throwing something in the bin that's already in the bin (DELETE, drop) doesn't throw it away twice.\n\n• Create enrolment, POST /enrolments: not idempotent, since each call creates a new enrolment resource, so repeating it would create duplicate enrolments.\n\n• View enrolment, GET /enrolments/{id}: idempotent, and also safe, since repeated reads never change server state and return the same representation until something else changes it.\n\n• Update tutorial timeslot, PUT /enrolments/{id}/timeslot: idempotent, since repeating the identical update leaves the enrolment in the same end state no matter how many times it's applied.\n\n• Drop course, DELETE /enrolments/{id}: idempotent, since deleting an already-deleted enrolment is a no-op — the enrolment is (still) gone either way.\n\n• A common mistake is: using PUT for create or POST for the timeslot update swaps the methods' real meanings; claiming GET changes state or is never cacheable gets GET's whole definition backwards; and claiming DELETE or PUT are not idempotent ignores that repeating either one leaves the resource in exactly the same final state.\n\nSo the answer is: POST (create, not idempotent), GET (view, idempotent/safe), PUT (update timeslot, idempotent), DELETE (drop, idempotent)."
