      "modelAnswer": "It's like a vending machine that only lights up the buttons for drinks it actually still has in stock, instead of printing every button on the front panel whether or not it works — you just press whatever's lit, instead of memorising which slots run out when.\n\n• What HATEOAS actually is: the fourth part of REST's uniform interface, where the response itself embeds the links/actions currently available, rather than the client having to know the business rules in advance.\n\n• How it applies here: GET /enrolments/{id} should include a hypermedia link to the drop action, with its URI and method, only while dropping is currently allowed (before census date); once census date passes, that link simply isn't in the response any more.\n\n• Why this is better than hard-coding: the client just checks whether a \"drop\" link is present to decide whether to show the button, instead of encoding the census-date rule itself and discovering it was wrong only when a drop attempt comes back with an error.\n\n• A common mistake is: always including the link regardless of eligibility is exactly the hard-coded-button problem the question asks to avoid, not a HATEOAS violation to omit it; having the client separately look up and compute the census-date rule itself re-creates the same hard-coding problem HATEOAS exists to solve; and relying purely on an error status after the fact is the \"try it and handle the error\" approach the question explicitly says to avoid.\n\nSo the answer is: include the drop link in the response only while dropping is actually allowed, and have the client check for the link's presence rather than hard-coding the rule."
