      "modelAnswer": "It's like asking a friend whether anything's changed on the group chat since you last checked — if the answer is 'nope, still the same,' you don't need them to re-read you the whole chat history, 'nope' is enough.\n\n• What an ETag actually is: a short fingerprint, like a version stamp, that the server attaches to a resource's current representation.\n\n• What an identical ETag on both calls shows: the enrolment resource's representation genuinely hasn't changed between the two requests.\n\n• How to use it to save work: the admin tool sends the ETag it already has back in an If-None-Match header on the second request; if the server finds the fingerprint still matches, it returns an empty-bodied response telling the tool its cached copy is still valid, instead of resending and re-parsing the full representation again.\n\n• A common mistake is: an ETag says nothing about whether a connection stayed open, it's a content fingerprint, not a connection indicator; a matching ETag doesn't lock a resource against future edits, it's just a snapshot comparison; and ETags have nothing to do with which authentication scheme is in use.\n\nSo the answer is: the matching ETag shows the representation hasn't changed, and the admin tool can send it back via If-None-Match on the next request to get an empty 'not modified' response instead of re-downloading the full data."
