      "modelAnswer": "It's like ordering food only when you're actually hungry, rather than keeping a full kitchen staffed and running around the clock just in case you want a meal once a year at an unpredictable moment.\n\n• What makes workload (1) a serverless fit: it sits at a low, steady background load almost all year, except for one extreme, unpredictable spike in the first hour enrolment opens, then drops back to near-zero for weeks.\n\n• Why serverless matches this shape: the course explicitly lists spiky/unpredictable load as a good fit for serverless, because functions scale dynamically to demand, even down to zero, and are billed only per invocation/execution time.\n\n• The cost argument against permanent provisioning: keeping VMs or containers running 24/7 at peak capacity means paying for that capacity constantly, for a burst that only actually happens once a semester — exactly the \"steady high load is often cheaper on VMs/containers; spiky load favours serverless\" trade-off the course draws.\n\n• A common mistake is: serverless isn't always more expensive, its whole appeal here is that it isn't; picking it just because it's newer isn't a real justification; and serverless doesn't require the customer to manage the OS at all, that's actually one of its defining features, so that claim gets it backwards.\n\nSo the answer is: Serverless (FaaS), because it matches a spiky, unpredictable load pattern without paying for idle peak capacity year-round."
